	LILHEX [0]

lilhex0 is the first in a set of tools for making an OS. It is a handwritten
selection of bytes (thought process can be read using the assembly listing
in NOTES) which shall execute as an elf64 amd64 linux executable. It takes
(via stdin) a set of hexedecimal values each followed by a whitespace
(IMPORTANT NOTE: source files must end in whitespace) which shall be returned
(via stdout) as literal bytes.

The single exception to this input syntax is the comment, which begins with
the ASCII character 0x23 "#", and ends with ASCII character 0x0A LF
(line feed).

Therefore, the file [ignore first tab of each line] which follows shall output
"HI!" to the terminal, followed by a carriage return and line feed.

	# HI!
	48 49 21
	# \r\n
	0D 0A

Note that non-comment non-hexdig characters shall be treated *AS THOUGH* they
are ASCII hexadecimal digits. Thus the text 'GG' becomes:

	ASCII 'G' == 0x47
	0x47 -> 0x10
	0x10 -> 0x00

Which produced an invalid output, without explanation.
